data: {"id":"20261001202054475fab554fe74dd2","created":1790857254,"object":"chat.completion.chunk","model":"glm-5.3-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_61b5a51947ca42a496b0472a","index":0,"type":"function","function":{"name":"write","arguments":"{\"content\":\"VERIFICATION BOUNDS AND COVERAGE\\n================================\\nClaim:  For all integers x, (x + 1) * (x - 1) = x^2 - 1\\nSpec:   forall x : Int. (x + 1) * (x - 1) = x * x - 1   (spec.txt)\\n\\n1. DOMAIN OF THE CLAIM\\n----------------------\\nQuantification: universally quantified over all integers x.\\n    Domain = Z = { ..., -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, ... }\\nThe domain is infinite and unbounded in both directions. No range\\nrestriction appears in the claim itself; any finite test set covers\\na measure-zero fraction of it.\\n\\n2. PROOF METHOD\\n---------------\\nSymbolic, deductive proof (proof.txt):\\n  - Method: equational rewriting for an arbitrary (universally\\n    quantified, non-numeric) x, closed by universal generalization.\\n  - Axiom base (permitted set):\\n      A1 commutativity of addition\\n      A2 commutativity of multiplication\\n      A3 distributivity of * over +\\n      A4 definition of subtraction (a - b = a + (-b))\\n  - Declared auxiliary definitional facts: a*1 = a, a + 0 = a,\\n    a + (-a) = 0 (the last is part of A4 as stated).\\n  - Scope of validity: because the derivation uses only the ring\\n    identities above, it is valid for EVERY element of every\\n    commutative ring with the stated identity laws - in particular\\n    for every integer, with no bound on magnitude. The proof is\\n    unbounded: it covers the entire domain Z, including values far\\n    outside any tested range, subject only to the audit caveat that\\n    the two identity laws (a*1 = a, a + 0 = a) are declared rather\\n    than derived (audit.txt, finding F2 / obligation OB-5).\\n\\n3. EMPIRICAL CHECK (counterexample_check.py / counterexample_check.txt)\\n-----------------------------------------------------------------------\\n  - Coverage: exactly the 201 integers x with -100 <= x <= 100.\\n  - Result: 0 counterexamples; claim holds for all tested values.\\n  - This check is CORROBORATIVE ONLY. It does not and cannot\\n    establish the universal claim (see limitations).\\n\\n4. LIMITATIONS OF THE CHECKER\\n-----------------------------\\nL1  Finite coverage: the empirical checker tests 201 of infinitely\\n    many integers. Agreement on [-100, 100] does not imply the\\n    claim for |x| > 100.\\nL2  Fixed-width arithmetic hazard: the checker used Python ints\\n    (arbitrary precision), so no overflow occurred. A checker built\\n    on fixed-width integers (e.g. 32-bit) could produce spurious\\n    counterexamples or mask real ones for large |x| via wraparound.\\n    Any future rerun with bounded machine types must account for this.\\nL3  Trusted code base: the checker's comparison (lhs != rhs) and the\\n    evaluation of both sides share Python's own integer semantics;\\n    a defect in the interpreter would go undetected. The empirical\\n    result is only as trustworthy as the runtime it ran on.\\nL4  Proof checker scope (checker.py): verification of proof.txt is\\n    textual/structural - it checks that each cited step label matches\\n    the permitted axiom set and that the step equations are well-formed\\n    rewrites. It does not independently re-derive the arithmetic; a\\n    malformed but well-labelled step could pass. Fully mechanical\\n    validation would require porting the derivation to a proof\\n    assistant (Lean, Coq, Isabelle) with a kernel-checked core.\\nL5  Axiom trust: the certificate assumes the standard axiomatization\\n    of the integers (ring axioms plus identity laws). It does not\\n    construct the integers or prove consistency; the audit (audit.txt)\\n    confirms only that nothing beyond the declared base is used.\\n\\n5. OVERALL COVERAGE STATEMENT\\n-----------------------------\\n  - Deductive coverage: